Advantages of using Mongoose over native MongoDB driver
Schema-based structure:
Mongoose lets you define schemas, while MongoDB driver is schema-less.
Data validation:
Built-in validation (required, min, max, etc.)
Middleware support:
You can run functions before/after operations (hooks).
Easier querying:
Cleaner and more readable syntax.
Relationships handling:
Supports population (similar to joins).
Model abstraction:
Works with models instead of raw collections.

Example:
Without Mongoose:
db.collection("users").insertOne({...})

With Mongoose:
User.create({...})

Difference between findOneAndUpdate() and updateOne()

findOneAndUpdate():

Finds a document AND updates it
Returns the document (old or new)
Can use option { new: true } to get updated doc

Example:
User.findOneAndUpdate(
{ name: "Abhishek" },
{ age: 22 },
{ new: true }
)

updateOne():

Updates only (does NOT return updated document)
Returns result object (acknowledged, modifiedCount)

Example:
User.updateOne(
{ name: "Abhishek" },
{ age: 22 }
)

Key Difference:

findOneAndUpdate → returns document
updateOne → returns status/result
Purpose of Middleware in Mongoose

Middleware (hooks) are functions that run:

Before (pre)
After (post)

Used for:

Validation
Logging
Password hashing
Data transformation

Example:
userSchema.pre("save", function(next) {
console.log("Before saving user");
next();
});

userSchema.post("save", function(doc) {
console.log("User saved:", doc);
});

Pagination in Mongoose

Using skip() and limit():

Example:
const page = 1;
const limit = 10;

const users = await User.find()
.skip((page - 1) * limit)
.limit(limit);

Explanation:

skip() → skips previous records
limit() → number of records per page

Optional response:
{
total: totalUsers,
page: 1,
limit: 10,
data: users
}

Embedding vs Referencing in MongoDB

Embedding (Nested Documents):

Store related data inside one document

Example:
{
name: "Abhishek",
orders: [
{ item: "Book", price: 100 }
]
}

Use when:

Data is tightly related
Read operations are frequent
Data size is small

Referencing (Using ObjectId):

Store references to another collection

Example:
{
name: "Abhishek",
orders: [ObjectId("123")]
}

Use when:

Data is large
Data is shared across multiple documents
Frequent updates required

Key Difference:

Embedding:

Faster reads
No joins needed
Can grow large

Referencing:

More flexible
Avoids duplication
Requires population (like joins)
